But wait — in `_tainted_vars` for the strict handler body, `e.data` usage: `document.getElementById('msg').textContent = e.data;` — ASSIGN matches var=`textContent`? `([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*=\s*` — `.textContent = e.data` → var=`textContent`, rhs=`e.data` → tainted via extra_sources. Then textContent is not a sink → no finding. Good.

For xssgame/postmessage-weak: body line `if (evt.origin.indexOf('example.com') === -1) { return; }` — ASSIGN: `===` — name before `=`? `('example.com') === -1` — name `com` then `') ` — `\s*=` needs `=` right after optional spaces; after `com` comes `'` → fail. Hmm what about `indexOf('example.com') === -1`? finditer scans all positions; `=== -1` — name must be `[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*` immediately before `\s*=\s*`. Before `===` is `)`. So no. Good.

`document.write(evt.data);` — sink document.write, line has data_expr → finding. 

dvwa/medium: `var q = location.search.substring(1).split('=')[1];` → q tainted (location.search). `document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = decodeURIComponent(q);` — sink `.innerHTML`, line has tainted q → finding. Also ASSIGN matches var=`innerHTML`, rhs=`decodeURIComponent(q)` → tainted. Fine.

xssgame/safe-textcontent: `var hash = location.hash.slice(1);` tainted hash; `textContent = hash` — no sink. No findings. 

webgoat/dom-eval: `var code = 'var x = "' + location.hash.slice(1) + '"';` — ASSIGN var=code rhs contains location.hash → tainted. Also inner `var x = "` — finditer: first match `var code = 'var x = "' + location...`? ASSIGN regex `(?:\b(?:var|let|const)\s+)?([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*=\s*([^;]+)` — matches at `var code = ...` with rhs up to `;`. rhs = `'var x = "' + location.hash.slice(1) + '"'` → tainted code. `eval(code);` → sink eval, tainted → finding. 

Now also generic scan of handler bodies: note that handler bodies are also part of the whole-script lines scan with extra_sources=[] — `document.write(evt.data)` line: sink present, taint? `evt.data` not a generic source, no tainted vars → no generic finding. Good, no duplicates.

But dvwa/low.html `document.write` line is inside script — generic scan catches it. Good.

One more check: `classify_origin_check` strict second regex: `(?:!==|===|!=|==)[^;]*origin` — for strict handler: `if (e.origin !== 'https://trusted.example.com') { return; }` — first regex: `origin\s*(?:!==...)` → `e.origin !==` matches → strict. Good.

Weak: `evt.origin.indexOf('example.com') === -1` — first regex: `evt\s*\.\s*origin\b` followed by `\s*(?:!==|===...)` — after origin comes `.indexOf` → no. Second: `(?:!==|===|!=|==)[^;]*evt\s*\.\s*origin` — is there `===` before an origin occurrence without `;` between? `=== -1) { return;` — `;` at return. `[^;]*` can't cross. Before that, no `===`. → weak. 

Array includes strict: `['https://a.com'].includes(e.origin)` → third regex. OK.

Edge: `==` loose equality classified strict — acceptable (still an exact comparison).

Now write fixtures and tests.

